      "body": "\n        <h2>Torque class</h2>\n        <p>MOZA rates the R9 V3 at 9 Nm peak torque and the R12 V2 at 12 Nm. Both use 21-bit encoders and MOZA's NexGen 4.0 force-feedback platform.</p>\n        <h2>Why 9 Nm is already a lot</h2>\n        <p>For many home racers, 9 Nm is enough to produce convincing steering weight while leaving usable detail in the signal. It is also easier to live with on lighter cockpits.</p>\n        <h2>What 12 Nm buys you</h2>\n        <p>The benefit is not just stronger steering. Extra torque gives more headroom for sharp peaks and allows you to run heavier wheels without making the base feel muted.</p>\n        <h2>Rig requirements</h2>\n        <p>The more torque you buy, the more important chassis rigidity becomes. A wheelbase cannot deliver clean detail if the mounting plate and seat are flexing underneath you.</p>\n        <h2>Verdict</h2>\n        <p>R9 V3 for value and most home setups; R12 V2 for a more serious rigid rig where the extra headroom will actually be used.</p><h2>Dynamic range is the real reason to buy more torque</h2><p>Higher torque is useful because you can run a comfortable normal steering weight while preserving sharper peaks above it. That extra headroom can make curb strikes, compression and loaded corners feel more distinct instead of flattening at the motor's limit.</p><h2>Heavier wheels change the equation</h2><p>A larger or heavier rim can make a lower-torque base feel softer. Drivers who rotate between formula and larger GT or round wheels have a better reason to value the R12's extra headroom than someone using one light wheel all the time.</p><h2>Spend on the structure first</h2><p>If the wheel deck flexes, 12 Nm can simply make the flex more obvious. Before moving up in torque, make sure the cockpit, seat and mounting plate can transmit the forces cleanly.</p><h2>Software strength should be tuned, not endured</h2><p>Owning a 12 Nm base does not mean every car should be run at maximum force. Set a comfortable steering weight and use the additional ceiling as headroom for detail. Excessive force can make long sessions tiring and can hide subtle information under constant heavy steering.</p><h2>Safety and ergonomics rise with torque</h2><p>Stronger bases can react sharply when the car crashes or the simulation sends a sudden peak. Keep hands clear when resetting, use sensible force settings and make sure children or visitors do not treat a powered direct-drive wheel like a toy.</p><h2>Upgrade path</h2><p>The R9 is a natural destination for someone leaving entry direct drive. The R12 is easier to justify when the cockpit is already permanent, the pedals are sorted and the driver wants to keep the same base through heavier wheels and stronger settings.</p>",
