      "body": "<h2>What Razer is selling here</h2><p>The Viper V4 Pro is a focused esports mouse rather than a do-everything desktop mouse. Razer's current specifications list a 49g black version, Focus Pro 50K optical sensor, Gen-4 optical switches and up to 8,000Hz polling in both wired and wireless modes.</p><p>That spec sheet is deliberately extreme. The practical question is not whether the hardware is fast enough—it clearly is—but whether the shape, weight and price make sense for you.</p><h2>Weight and shape matter more than 50,000 DPI</h2><p>No sensible player needs to run a mouse at 50,000 DPI. The meaningful benefits are the light shell, low-friction feet, predictable symmetrical shape and a wireless implementation designed around low latency. Competitive players making frequent fast corrections can appreciate low mass far more than the headline sensor number.</p><h2>Battery life is unusually strong for this class</h2><p>Razer claims up to 180 hours at 1,000Hz and up to 45 hours at 8,000Hz. High polling rates cost battery and can add CPU overhead, so there is no shame in using 1,000Hz or 2,000Hz if the rest of the system does not benefit from 8,000Hz.</p><h2>What independent reviewers are saying</h2><p>PC Gamer currently ranks the Viper V4 Pro as its best overall gaming mouse for 2026, praising the build quality, low weight, performance and battery life while calling out the high price and lack of extras such as RGB. That is a useful framing: this is premium because the fundamentals are refined, not because it has the longest feature list.</p><h2>Who should not buy it?</h2><p>If you play MMOs, work all day in spreadsheets or love a thumb full of shortcuts, a G502-style mouse can be more useful. If you are casual, a cheaper modern wireless mouse can already be accurate enough that the Viper's extra performance headroom goes unnoticed.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>The Viper V4 Pro looks like one of the best competitive mice available, but it is a specialised luxury. Buy it for shape, weight, build and wireless performance—not because a 50K sensor or 8K polling figure sounds like an automatic skill upgrade.</p>",
