      "slug": "helldivers-2-beginner-guide",
      "image": "assets/images/helldivers-beginner.svg",
      "category": "HELLDIVERS 2 GUIDE",
      "title": "Helldivers 2 Beginner Guide: The Habits That Make Squads Better",
      "excerpt": "Friendly fire, objectives, reinforcement discipline and why running away is occasionally the most patriotic option.",
      "score": "8 MIN",
      "art": "game",
      "quick": "Stay near the squad without standing on top of them, call reinforcement beacons into safe areas, learn stratagem placement, complete objectives before chasing every fight, and remember that disengaging can save more time than winning a pointless battle.",
      "body": "\n        <h2>Friendly fire is always part of the equation</h2>\n        <p>Your weapons and stratagems can kill teammates. Give explosives room, announce dangerous call-ins when possible and avoid firing through another diver just because an enemy is somewhere behind them.</p>\n        <h2>Objectives matter more than kill count</h2>\n        <p>Enemies can keep arriving. Completing the mission, secondary objectives and useful map tasks is generally more important than fighting every patrol you encounter.</p>\n        <h2>Reinforce intelligently</h2>\n        <p>Throw reinforcement beacons toward safe ground and, when practical, near the fallen player's equipment. Tossing someone directly into the middle of a swarm is technically a deployment strategy, just not a particularly kind one.</p>\n        <h2>Know when to disengage</h2>\n        <p>A squad can burn through reinforcements fighting for ground that has no strategic value. Breaking contact and moving to the next objective is often the stronger choice.</p><h2>Objectives beat kill counts</h2><p>The mission is usually won by moving, completing tasks and extracting—not by clearing every patrol. Fighting everything consumes time, ammunition and reinforcements while spawning more problems.</p><h2>Call stratagems before the situation is desperate</h2><p>Support weapons, resupplies and defensive tools are easier to use when the squad still controls the area. Waiting until everyone is surrounded turns useful tools into panic buttons.</p><h2>Friendly fire is a positioning problem</h2><p>Before throwing an orbital or firing through a teammate, know where the squad is moving. The game is intentionally lethal to allies, so spacing and communication are core mechanics rather than etiquette.</p><h2>Build a loadout that covers jobs, not just damage</h2><p>A squad benefits when someone can deal with armour, someone can clear crowds and someone brings utility such as resupply, sentries or defensive tools. Four players copying the same favourite weapon can leave the team helpless against a different threat type.</p><h2>Samples change how you should die</h2><p>Samples are shared when extracted, so protecting the dropped sample container can matter more than the individual who carried it. If a teammate falls near the extraction route, recovering the samples is useful; sprinting deep into a hopeless fight for one ordinary weapon often is not.</p><h2>Higher difficulty rewards cleaner decisions</h2><p>As difficulty rises, unnecessary fights become more expensive. Learn objective flow, recognise when a position is collapsing and use reinforcement budget as a team resource. The squad that disengages intelligently often finishes with more rewards than the squad that tries to win every local battle.</p>",
